.TH CVEC_AT 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cvec_at \- access element at index (type-inferred, returns reference)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cvector.h>

element_ref = cvec_at(v, index);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro is a type-inferred wrapper for cvector_at(). It gives a
reference to the element rather than a pointer, cast to the correct type
automatically, so you can use it as an lvalue in an assignment.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
v      Vector to access
index  Zero-based index of the element
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Gives the element reference at the index
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro gives a reference, not a pointer.
The macro evaluates index exactly once, so an expression with a side
effect, for example cvec_at(v, i++), is safe to pass.
The macro stops the program with ccol_fatal_err() when the index is out
of bounds.
You can use the macro on the left side of an assignment.
For an alternative that gives NULL for an index that is out of bounds
instead of stopping the program, use cvec_at_ptr.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cvec_construct(vec, int);
cvec_push(vec, 42);
int val = cvec_at(vec, 0);  // val == 42
cvec_at(vec, 0) = 5;        // the first element is now 5
cvec_destroy(vec);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cvector_at (3),
.BR cvec_at_ptr (3)
